  "live_records": {
    "q-80309": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80309",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-{(2x+4)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-(2x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7(1-(2x+4)^{2}/7)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(2x+4)^{2}/7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x+4)^{2}/7}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((2x+4)/\\sqrt{7})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7-(2x+4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((2x+4)/\\sqrt{7})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2x+4}{\\sqrt{7}})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
