    "q-80308": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80308",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{(6x-2)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-(6x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9(1-(6x-2)^{2}/9)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(6x-2)^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{1-(6x-2)^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x-2)^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x-2)/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{6x-2}{3}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{6x-2}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-(6x-2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((6x-2)/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6x-2}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
