    "q-3593": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3593",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+13 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+13 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+13 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+2)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+2}{3}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+2}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4x+13}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+2)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
