    "q-19535": {
      "dom_id": "step-q19535",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+5 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+5 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{(x-2)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-2.\n$$\n\n \nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{1+(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
