      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-12x+8 & =-2({x}^{2}+6x-4) \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+[{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}]-4) \\\\  & =-2({(x+3)}^{2}-13) \\\\  & =2(13-{(x+3)}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+18}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{2(13-(x+3)^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26(1-(x+3)^{2}/13)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x+3)^{2}/13)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+3)/\\sqrt{13})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{-2{x}^{2}-12x+18}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+3)/\\sqrt{13})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{26}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =11\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{13}})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
