    "q-270402": {
      "dom_id": "step-q270402",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-5}^{-3}\\frac{60}{|x+5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+10x+24 & ={x}^{2}+10x+({5}^{2}-{5}^{2})+24 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+10x+25)-25+24 \\\\  & =(x+5)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-5}^{-3}\\frac{60}{|x+5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-5}^{-3}\\frac{60}{|x+5|\\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+5.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\sqrt{2}-5$ | $-3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-5}^{-3}\\frac{60}{|x+5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-5}^{-3}\\frac{60}{|x+5|\\sqrt{(x+5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}\\frac{60}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =60\\text{arcsec }u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =60(\\text{arcsec}(2)-\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =60(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
